3
00:00:16.621 --> 00:00:27.592
<v NARRATOR>The forest went the color of tea at dusk, and three of the smallest neighbors on Earth gathered at a fallen log to do their evening work.

4
00:00:27.592 --> 00:00:41.363
<v NARRATOR>Photo rested in the last of the sunlight on a wet green leaf, Crumble sat in the soft heart of the log, and Net threaded through the dark soil beneath everything.

5
00:00:41.363 --> 00:00:49.054
<v NET>Evening, both of you — I am here below, as always, ready to carry.

6
00:00:49.054 --> 00:00:59.585
<v PHOTO>I begin the ring, since I always do — I take sunlight, water, and the carbon in the air and build them into living matter.

7
00:00:59.585 --> 00:01:07.036
<v PHOTO>But if the matter I build never came back, the world would run out of the raw stuff to build from.

8
00:01:07.036 --> 00:01:18.287
<v CRUMBLE>That is my part — when a leaf or a log finishes being alive, I unlock the good matter trapped inside the dead and free it back into the soil.

9
00:01:18.287 --> 00:01:26.498
<v CRUMBLE>I take what looks like over and make it available again, so nothing Photo built is ever lost for good.

10
00:01:26.498 --> 00:01:37.429
<v NET>And I carry — I gather the freed nutrients Crumble releases and thread them into the roots of the living, the seedling reaching in the shade.

11
00:01:37.429 --> 00:01:47.000
<v NARRATOR>A student crept close and asked the question the three had never settled — whose part is the most important?

12
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:52.851
<v PHOTO>Take me away, and there is nothing new to return.

13
00:01:52.851 --> 00:02:00.102
<v CRUMBLE>Take me away, and the matter locks in the dead forever, and Photo simply runs out.

14
00:02:00.102 --> 00:02:09.313
<v NET>Take me away, and the returned nutrients sit uselessly in the soil, and the new life never receives them.

15
00:02:09.313 --> 00:02:16.884
<v NARRATOR>The atoms in that new sprout were an old tree once — nothing here was ever made from nothing.

16
00:02:16.884 --> 00:02:31.015
<v NARRATOR>One begins it, one returns it, one carries it on, and the ring has no end, only a next beginning. That is the great return.

17
